{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/604847-requiem-1st-edition-questions-about-larp-systems","id":604847,"title":"(Requiem 1st Edition) Questions About LARP Systems","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-07-27T11:13:44-07:00","id":604847,"title":"(Requiem 1st Edition) Questions About LARP Systems","content":"So, I have considered doing a LARP in the early 1st Requiem style, setting, and rules, but I have some questions about the various possibilities I could do for the rules system. Is the original MET rules for nWoD worth it? From what I have heard, it is similar enough to the tabletop rules that you can backport the 1e tabletop corebooks of the other gamelines into the MET system very easily, (which is good, because from what I have read, only Requiem and the blue Mortals corebook have been adapted to the nWoD MET), but the one thing I am wary of is the resolution mechanic of drawing cards.<br /> <br /> I am more familiar with oWoD LARP and its Rock-Paper-Scissors approach and I think I would like that better than drawing playing cards, but I am unsure. Is the card system really good or should I build my own homebrew live-action system for 1e nWoD? How similar is the published system to the tabletop system? Any personal advice for using the published rules in general?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1579,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1579-camilla","user":"Camilla","date":". 07-27-2015, 02:15 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1579-camilla","name":"Camilla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1579_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1044"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-27T11:52:47-07:00","id":604887,"title":"","content":"I played a lot of Requiem LARP a few years back with the MET system. By far the biggest advantage of the system is that it's dead simple to convert any of the tabletop rules to the MET system. The biggest disadvantage is that the system encourages min-maxing <i>hardcore</i>. I never found drawing cards to be a problem, and if you do find it slows down play, you can always use a single d10 in a clear plastic case instead, or a dice rolling app if anyone has smartphones. Personally, I found the benefits of the system's ease of use and cross-compatibility to be well worth the trade off, especially with a group who's willing to roleplay in good faith and build real characters not just collections of dots. If you do use it, I would highly recommend lowering the success thresholds to 8 for the first success and 3 for each additional successes, as opposed to the default 10 for the first and 5 for each additional. Also, cap successes at 5 in combat.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-27T13:47:12-07:00","id":604940,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/604847-requiem-1st-edition-questions-about-larp-systems?p=604887#post604887\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I played a lot of Requiem LARP a few years back with the MET system. By far the biggest advantage of the system is that it's dead simple to convert any of the tabletop rules to the MET system. The biggest disadvantage is that the system encourages min-maxing <i>hardcore</i>. I never found drawing cards to be a problem, and if you do find it slows down play, you can always use a single d10 in a clear plastic case instead, or a dice rolling app if anyone has smartphones. Personally, I found the benefits of the system's ease of use and cross-compatibility to be well worth the trade off, especially with a group who's willing to roleplay in good faith and build real characters not just collections of dots. If you do use it, I would highly recommend lowering the success thresholds to 8 for the first success and 3 for each additional successes, as opposed to the default 10 for the first and 5 for each additional. Also, cap successes at 5 in combat.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sounds excellent and worth checking out. I think I am sold on this system if what you are saying about converting tabletop material is true.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1579-camilla","name":"Camilla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1579_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1044"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}